{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Bless, my soul, O YHWH.</b> By reason, this psalm too is David's, for its beginning and end match the first. And in this psalm he recounts the work of Genesis, beginning with light, for it was first, and afterward the heavens and the earth and the vegetation and the luminaries and the fish of the sea and beasts of the field, man and the blessing. That the soul should bless is a sod: it gives thanks and says to the Name, for He is very great, in the manner of 'and exalted above every blessing and praise.'" "<b>And the meaning of 'clothed in honor and splendor.'</b> Because of the abundance of Your honor and splendor, the eyes of the wise soul are prevented from seeing Your glory, just as a garment prevents seeing the essence of the one clothed."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 104, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:38:08.397895587Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}